STANCES=[
 (130, "Speculative decoding changes the arithmetic intensity of decode and therefore changes which parallel axis is cheap.",
  "Speculative decoding replaces one-token-at-a-time decode with a draft-then-verify loop in which the target model scores several candidate tokens in a single forward pass. That converts a chain of tiny, latency-bound steps into fewer, wider steps, which raises arithmetic intensity and amortises each layer's collectives over more accepted tokens. Tensor parallelism, whose cost is a fixed per-layer synchronous all-reduce, is repaid better under speculation than under plain decode. Pipeline parallelism sees the opposite pressure: the verify step is still one logical request walking every stage, so the bubble is unchanged while the per-step work grows.",
  "H130: enabling speculative decoding will shrink the TTFT-normalised TPOT penalty attributed to tensor parallelism by a larger relative margin than it shrinks the penalty attributed to pipeline parallelism, at fixed acceptance rate (ESTIMATE; derivation: TP collective cost per layer is roughly constant per forward pass, so cost per accepted token falls with the mean accepted-token count, whereas the PP bubble fraction (PP-1)/(micro_batches+PP-1) does not depend on tokens accepted per pass).",
  "Run four arms: TP-only and PP-only, each with speculation off and on, holding draft model, acceptance threshold, batching policy and quantisation fixed. Log mean accepted tokens per verify pass per arm and normalise all latency numbers by it.",
  ["Mean and distribution of accepted tokens per verification pass, per arm",
   "TTFT p95 and TPOT p95 for all four cells with run-to-run variance across at least three trials",
   "Per-layer collective time and per-step device time separated from host time",
   "Draft model identity, size and acceptance threshold recorded per arm"],
  ["Acceptance rate is workload-dependent, so a gain measured on one prompt mix may not transfer",
   "Speculation raises tail variance because rejected drafts waste a full target forward pass",
   "Comparing speculation-on against speculation-off while also changing parallel degree confounds two variables"],
  0.6),
 (131, "Quantisation shifts the bottleneck from memory bandwidth toward collectives, which penalises tensor parallelism relative to its unquantised baseline.",
  "In decode the dominant device cost is streaming weights out of HBM, and weight-only quantisation reduces those bytes roughly in proportion to the bit-width ratio. Collectives, however, operate on activations, which are usually kept at higher precision and are unaffected. So quantisation shrinks the numerator of the compute term while leaving the tensor-parallel synchronisation term intact, raising the fraction of step time spent in all-reduce. A tensor-parallel degree that looked free at full precision can become the visible bottleneck once weights are compressed.",
  "H131: moving from full-precision to weight-only quantised weights will increase the share of per-token step time spent in all-reduce for the tensor-parallel arm, and the absolute TPOT gain from quantisation will be smaller at higher tensor degree than at lower tensor degree (ESTIMATE; derivation: per-GPU weight bytes scale as total_weight_bytes / TP and shrink further by the quantisation ratio, while activation all-reduce payload is hidden_size * dtype_bytes per layer and is invariant to weight precision, so the ratio of collective time to compute time rises).",
  "Sweep tensor degree over at least two values, crossed with quantised and unquantised weights, four cells total, holding batching policy, speculation setting and sequence-length distribution fixed. Capture a collective-level profile in every cell.",
  ["Per-cell breakdown of step time into GEMM, attention, all-reduce and host time",
   "Achieved HBM read bandwidth per cell, to confirm the memory-bound assumption",
   "Output quality check on a held-out set to confirm quantisation did not degrade the model beyond the accepted bound",
   "Exact quantisation scheme, group size and which tensors were left at higher precision"],
  ["Quantisation changes model outputs, so a latency win can hide an accuracy regression",
   "Kernel availability differs by quantisation scheme and tensor degree, so a cell may be slow for implementation reasons rather than architectural ones",
   "Activation precision policy is often implicit and must be recorded or the comparison is not reproducible"],
  0.6),
 (132, "The prefill and decode phases prefer opposite parallel layouts, so a single global choice is a compromise unless the phases are disaggregated.",
  "Prefill is a large, compute-dense batched matrix multiply over the whole prompt: it has high arithmetic intensity and tolerates collectives well, so it scales acceptably with tensor degree. Decode is a sequence of thin, memory-bound steps in which the same collective is paid per layer per token and is latency-bound. Optimising one layout for both phases means accepting a setting that is wrong for at least one of them. Prefill-decode disaggregation exists precisely to let each phase pick its own degree, at the cost of transferring KV state between the two pools.",
  "H132: the tensor degree that minimises TTFT will not equal the tensor degree that minimises TPOT for the same model and hardware, and the gap will widen as the prompt-to-output length ratio grows (ESTIMATE; derivation: TTFT is dominated by a compute-bound prefill whose time falls with added parallel compute, while TPOT is dominated by per-layer collective latency that rises with parallel degree, so the two objectives move in opposite directions in the same sweep).",
  "Sweep tensor degree across the available range in a single-node aggregated deployment and report TTFT p95 and TPOT p95 separately per degree, at two prompt-to-output ratios. Do not report a blended latency number.",
  ["TTFT p95 and TPOT p95 curves against tensor degree, reported separately, at two length ratios",
   "Prompt and output length distributions actually served in each run, not the intended ones",
   "If disaggregation is evaluated, the measured KV transfer time and the fabric it crossed",
   "Per-phase GPU utilisation to confirm prefill is compute-bound and decode is memory-bound"],
  ["A blended latency metric hides the conflict and will produce a layout that satisfies neither SLO",
   "Disaggregation adds a KV transfer on the critical path and a new failure mode if the pools scale independently",
   "Length distributions drift in production, moving the optimum after the decision is made"],
  0.6),
 (133, "Continuous batching couples the parallelism choice to the scheduler, so layout cannot be evaluated with a fixed batch size.",
  "Under continuous batching, requests join and leave the running batch at token granularity, so the effective batch a given decode step sees is a moving quantity set by arrival rate, admission policy and preemption. Tensor parallelism pays its collective once per layer per step regardless of how many sequences are in the step, so its cost per token falls as the running batch grows. Pipeline parallelism instead needs enough concurrent micro-batches to fill stages, which the scheduler may or may not supply. Benchmarking either layout at a pinned batch size measures a regime the production scheduler never occupies.",
  "H133: the ranking of tensor-parallel against pipeline-parallel layouts measured under a fixed batch size will invert at some arrival rate under continuous batching, because the two layouts' costs scale differently with the running batch size (ESTIMATE; derivation: TP per-token collective cost scales as constant/batch, while PP bubble fraction depends on the number of in-flight micro-batches; these are different functions of concurrency, so their difference can change sign).",
  "Drive both arms with the same open-loop arrival process at several offered loads spanning underload to near saturation, recording the running-batch-size distribution per arm, and additionally run one closed-loop fixed-batch arm for contrast. Do not compare across different load generators.",
  ["Running batch size distribution over time per arm, not just its mean",
   "Offered load, achieved throughput and queueing delay separated from execution latency",
   "TTFT p95 and TPOT p95 as functions of offered load for both arms",
   "Scheduler configuration including admission policy, preemption policy and any chunked-prefill setting"],
  ["Closed-loop load generators cap concurrency and hide saturation behaviour",
   "Preemption and recomputation events distort tail latency and are easy to omit from reporting",
   "A layout chosen at one arrival rate may be wrong after traffic growth, so the decision needs a re-evaluation trigger"],
  0.6),
 (134, "KV cache capacity, not compute, usually decides how far tensor parallelism must go, and capacity is a latency variable through the preemption path.",
  "Tensor parallelism divides both weight bytes and KV bytes across ranks, so raising the degree buys KV headroom. That headroom is not a comfort metric: when the cache is exhausted the scheduler must preempt or recompute, and those events show up as large latency spikes in exactly the tail the SLO governs. Pipeline parallelism divides layers, which also divides KV per device, but along an axis that leaves a request serialised across stages. The right framing is that layout choice sets the concurrency at which the system starts thrashing.",
  "H134: the tensor degree that satisfies the TPOT p95 SLO at target concurrency will be set by the onset of KV-driven preemption rather than by collective cost, and the SLO breach will correlate with preemption events rather than with a smooth latency drift (ESTIMATE; derivation: KV bytes grow linearly with concurrency times sequence length while available KV bytes are fixed per layout, so there is a threshold concurrency beyond which admission fails; preemption cost is a step change, not a gradual one).",
  "For each layout, ramp concurrency until the SLO breaks, and record preemption and recomputation counters alongside latency percentiles. Report the concurrency at first breach and whether preemption counters were non-zero at that point.",
  ["Per-layout KV cache capacity in tokens, derived and stated with the derivation",
   "Preemption and recomputation event counts aligned in time with latency percentiles",
   "Concurrency at first SLO breach per layout, with at least three repetitions",
   "Sequence-length distribution used in the ramp, since KV demand is length-driven"],
  ["Reporting mean latency will completely hide preemption spikes",
   "Cache capacity computed from a formula rather than read from the engine is an estimate and may exclude fragmentation and reserved blocks",
   "A layout tuned to the edge of the preemption threshold has no headroom for traffic growth or longer prompts"],
  0.6),
 (135, "Failure blast radius differs between the axes, and availability cost belongs in the same decision as latency.",
  "A tensor-parallel group is a synchronous collective domain: every rank participates in every all-reduce, so the loss of one GPU stalls and then fails the entire replica, and recovery means restarting all ranks. A pipeline-parallel deployment fails the replica too, but the stage boundary is a natural place to re-place a single stage, and stage-local restart is at least conceivable. Both are worse than replica-level redundancy, which is the actual availability mechanism. The point is that a larger collective domain concentrates risk and lengthens recovery, and that time is unavailability the SLO does not forgive.",
  "H135: mean time to restore service after a single-GPU fault will grow with tensor degree, because the number of ranks that must be re-initialised and the weight bytes that must be reloaded grow with the size of the collective domain (ESTIMATE; derivation: recovery time is dominated by process re-initialisation, NCCL communicator rebuild and weight reload across all ranks of the failed replica, and all three scale with the rank count in that replica).",
  "Inject a single-rank fault in each arm under steady load and measure time from fault to full SLO-compliant service, repeated at least three times per arm, with identical health-check and restart policy across arms.",
  ["Time from fault injection to first served request and to SLO-compliant service, three trials per arm",
   "Number of requests failed or dropped during recovery per arm",
   "NCCL communicator initialisation time and weight reload time separated in the recovery timeline",
   "Health check and restart policy configuration, identical across arms"],
  ["Fault injection in production is unsafe and must be confined to a staging replica or a drained canary",
   "Hung collectives may not surface as a clean process failure, so detection time can dominate recovery time",
   "Optimising latency alone can select a layout whose recovery time silently breaches the availability objective"],
  0.6),
 (136, "Multi-node crossings change the question entirely, because collective latency floors on the network fabric, not on NVLink.",
  "Inside a node, all-reduce on NVLink-class fabric has a small-message latency floor in the single-digit microseconds. Across nodes over RoCE or InfiniBand, the same small collective must traverse NICs, switches and the host stack unless GPUDirect RDMA keeps it off the host, and its latency floor rises by roughly an order of magnitude. Since decode pays two all-reduces per layer synchronously, a tensor-parallel group that spans nodes multiplies a large fixed cost by the layer count. Pipeline parallelism sends one hidden-state tensor per stage boundary per step, which is far fewer crossings, which is why pipeline is the conventional axis for crossing a node boundary.",
  "H136: extending a tensor-parallel group across a node boundary will increase TPOT by an amount proportional to the layer count times the inter-node small-message latency, and the same crossing used as a pipeline stage boundary will increase TPOT by roughly one such latency per boundary (ESTIMATE; derivation: TP incurs two all-reduces per layer, so L*2 crossings per token, while PP incurs (PP-1) point-to-point sends per token; the ratio is set by architecture, not by tuning).",
  "Compare an intra-node tensor group against the same tensor degree split across two nodes, and against a pipeline split at the node boundary, holding total GPU count, model and engine version fixed. Verify GPUDirect RDMA is actually active in the multi-node arms before attributing any result.",
  ["Small-message all-reduce latency measured on the actual fabric with a standalone collective benchmark, per arm",
   "Confirmation that GPUDirect RDMA is enabled and in use, from NCCL debug output rather than from configuration intent",
   "NCCL topology and algorithm selection logs for each arm",
   "TTFT p95 and TPOT p95 per arm with at least three trials"],
  ["A misconfigured fabric silently falls back to a host-staged path and produces results that indict the architecture rather than the configuration",
   "NCCL algorithm and protocol selection can change between arms and must be pinned or reported",
   "Congestion control and other tenants on a shared fabric make inter-node numbers non-reproducible unless the fabric state is recorded"],
  0.62),
 (137, "Chunked prefill mixes the two phases into one step, which changes the collective profile that the layout comparison is measuring.",
  "Chunked prefill splits a long prompt into pieces so that prefill work can be interleaved with ongoing decode steps rather than blocking them. The consequence for a layout comparison is that a step is no longer purely memory-bound decode or purely compute-bound prefill: it is a mixture whose composition depends on the chunk size and the arrival pattern. Tensor parallelism's per-layer collective is amortised over more work in a mixed step than in a pure decode step, so chunked prefill flatters tensor parallelism relative to a pure-decode benchmark. Any comparison that leaves chunking at a default is measuring the default, not the architecture.",
  "H137: turning chunked prefill on will reduce the measured TPOT penalty of the tensor-parallel arm relative to the pipeline-parallel arm, and the size of the reduction will depend monotonically on the chunk size over the practical range (ESTIMATE; derivation: mixing prefill work into a decode step raises the compute performed per collective, and the collective cost per layer is roughly fixed, so the collective share of step time falls as chunk size rises).",
  "Cross both layouts with chunked prefill off and on at two chunk sizes, holding arrival process, model, quantisation and speculation fixed. Report TTFT p95 and TPOT p95 separately because chunking trades one against the other.",
  ["Chunk size and chunked-prefill enablement recorded per cell, not assumed from defaults",
   "TTFT p95 and TPOT p95 per cell, reported separately",
   "Per-step composition, that is the fraction of steps containing prefill work, per cell",
   "Prompt length distribution, since chunking only matters for prompts longer than the chunk"],
  ["Chunked prefill improves decode latency at the cost of TTFT, so a single blended metric will mislead",
   "Default chunk sizes differ across engine versions, making cross-version comparisons invalid",
   "If the prompt distribution is short, chunking is inert and the experiment will show a null result for the wrong reason"],
  0.6),
 (138, "The decision should be made against a declared service level objective and a rejection rule, otherwise the benchmark cannot conclude anything.",
  "A comparison without a pre-declared decision rule degenerates into picking whichever arm looks better on whichever metric moved. The disciplined form is to state the SLO first, in percentile terms for both TTFT and TPOT, state the minimum effect size that would justify a change given deployment cost, state the number of trials needed for that effect to exceed run-to-run variance, and state in advance what result would cause the change to be rejected. Only then does running the arms produce information rather than a narrative.",
  "H138: the TPOT p95 difference between the tensor-parallel and pipeline-parallel arms at target concurrency will exceed the pre-declared minimum effect size in at least four of five independent trials; failure to reach that threshold is a decision to keep the incumbent layout, not a reason to run more trials (ESTIMATE; derivation: with run-to-run variance measured before the arms are compared, the minimum detectable effect follows from that variance and the trial count, so the threshold is set by measurement rather than by preference).",
  "Before running any arm, measure run-to-run variance of the incumbent layout across five repetitions, publish the minimum effect size and trial count, then run both arms interleaved rather than in blocks to absorb drift, and evaluate strictly against the pre-published rule.",
  ["Written SLO with percentile definitions and target concurrency, published before the runs",
   "Run-to-run variance of the incumbent measured across at least five repetitions",
   "Pre-declared minimum effect size and trial count, with the derivation from that variance",
   "Interleaved per-trial raw results, not only aggregates"],
  ["Post-hoc metric selection will manufacture a positive result from noise",
   "Block-ordered runs confound arm with time-varying host and fabric conditions",
   "An effect that is statistically visible but smaller than deployment risk is not a reason to change layout"],
  0.62),
 (139, "Engine and library versions are part of the layout, so a parallelism comparison is only valid within a pinned software stack.",
  "Kernel coverage, collective algorithm selection, attention implementation and scheduler heuristics all differ across engine and communication-library versions, and each of those differences lands unevenly on the two axes. A tensor-parallel arm may gain from a fused kernel that exists only in a newer build; a pipeline arm may gain from a scheduler change in micro-batch handling. If the arms are not run on byte-identical stacks, the experiment measures release history rather than architecture, and the conclusion will not survive the next upgrade.",
  "H139: repeating the same two-arm comparison on a different engine or collective-library version will change the measured gap by an amount comparable to the gap itself, which means any layout decision must be re-validated after an upgrade rather than inherited (ESTIMATE; derivation: the gap is a difference of two similar quantities, so version-level changes of a few percent in either arm's kernels or collective algorithms produce a much larger relative change in their difference).",
  "Pin engine version, collective library version, driver and container image, record all four, run both arms on that identical stack, then repeat the whole comparison on one upgraded stack to quantify version sensitivity before treating the result as durable.",
  ["Exact engine, collective library, driver and container image identifiers for every run",
   "Collective algorithm and protocol selection logs, to detect version-driven changes in behaviour",
   "The two-arm gap measured on at least two software stacks, to quantify version sensitivity",
   "A record of any flags that differ between arms, which should be none apart from the layout itself"],
  ["An unpinned stack makes a result unreproducible and unattributable",
   "Upgrades can silently invert a layout decision, so a decision without a re-validation trigger will decay",
   "Container images that float on a mutable tag defeat pinning even when the version string looks fixed"],
  0.62),
]
